---
import BlockLayout from '../layouts/BlockLayout.astro';
import book from '../data/canterbury.json';
import '../styles/canterbury.css';
const description = 'Enter an illustrated Canterbury Tales: meet six pilgrims, choose a reading path, follow the storytelling threads, and hear a little Middle English.';
const firstRoute = book.routes[0], firstConnection = book.connections[0], firstStop = book.journey[0];
const taleById = (id: string) => book.tales.find(tale => tale.id === id)!;
const art = '/images/canterbury/';
const imageSet = (id: string) => `${art}${id}-480.webp 480w, ${art}${id}-960.webp 960w, ${art}${id}.webp 1440w`;
const portraitSet = (id: string) => `${art}portrait-${id}-160.webp 160w, ${art}portrait-${id}-320.webp 320w, ${art}portrait-${id}.webp 640w`;
---
<BlockLayout title="The Canterbury Tales · A company of voices" description={description} image={`${art}hero.webp`} imageWidth={1536} imageHeight={1024} isolated immersive showNetworkStrip={false} themeColor="#f5efdf" alternates={[{type:'application/json',href:'/canterbury.json',title:'Canterbury reading paths and sources'}]} jsonLd={{'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'CreativeWork',name:'The Canterbury Tales · A company of voices',url:'https://pointcast.xyz/canterbury/',description,isAccessibleForFree:true,about:{'@type':'Book',name:'The Canterbury Tales',author:{'@type':'Person',name:'Geoffrey Chaucer'},inLanguage:'enm'},creator:{'@type':'Organization',name:'PointCast'},citation:book.sources.map(source=>source.url)}}>
<div class="ct" data-canterbury data-language="both">
  <a class="ct-skip" href="#company">Skip to the pilgrims</a>
  <header class="ct-nav"><a class="ct-wordmark" href="/">POINTCAST <span>the illuminated shelf</span></a><nav aria-label="Canterbury experience"><a href="/books/">The books</a><a href="#company">The company</a><a href="#sources">Sources</a></nav></header>
  <section class="ct-opening" aria-labelledby="ct-title">
    <div class="ct-opening-copy"><p class="ct-kicker">Geoffrey Chaucer / late fourteenth century</p><h1 id="ct-title">The<br/>Canterbury<br/><em>Tales.</em></h1><p class="ct-dek">One road.<br/>A very lively company.</p><p>Saints, swindlers, lovers, a talking rooster.<br/>Come for the pilgrimage. Stay for the argument.</p><a class="ct-button" href="#company">Meet your fellow travelers <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a><p class="ct-small">Six doorways into a much larger, unfinished book.<br/>Spoiler-light until you choose to turn the page.</p></div>
    <figure class="ct-hero-art"><img src={`${art}hero.webp`} srcset={`${art}hero-640.webp 640w, ${art}hero-1280.webp 1280w, ${art}hero.webp 1536w`} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 58vw" width="1536" height="1024" alt="Original painted interpretation of a colorful company of medieval pilgrims setting off through a richly imagined landscape" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"/><figcaption>AN IMAGINED PILGRIMAGE / ORIGINAL ART FOR POINTCAST</figcaption><span class="ct-seal" aria-hidden="true">A company<br/>of voices<br/><b>✳</b></span></figure>
  </section>
  <div class="ct-invitation"><span aria-hidden="true">❦</span><p>A story is different<br/><em>when someone else tells it.</em></p><div>Chaucer’s pilgrims gather at the Tabard Inn in Southwark, bound for Thomas Becket’s shrine at Canterbury. Their host proposes a storytelling contest. Soon, stories become replies, rivalries, and performances. <a href="#frame">A little context ↓</a></div></div>
  <section class="ct-section ct-company" id="company" aria-labelledby="company-title"><header class="ct-section-head"><div><p class="ct-kicker">I / Pull up a seat</p><h2 id="company-title">Choose your<br/><em>company.</em></h2></div><p>Six storytellers. Six ways of seeing.<br/><span class="ct-with-js">Select a portrait to meet the pilgrim.</span><span class="ct-without-js">Select a portrait to read the tale.</span> These are imaginative portraits of literary characters.</p></header>
    <ol class="ct-pilgrims">{book.tales.map((tale,i)=><li><a data-pilgrim={tale.id} href={`#tale-${tale.id}`}><span class="ct-portrait"><img src={tale.portrait} srcset={portraitSet(tale.id)} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 43vw, 15vw" width="640" height="640" alt={`Painted interpretation of ${tale.pilgrim}`} loading="lazy" decoding="async"/><span aria-hidden="true">0{i+1}</span></span><h3>{tale.pilgrim}</h3><p>{tale.voice}</p><span class="ct-link">Meet the storyteller <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></span></a></li>)}</ol>
  </section>
  <section class="ct-section ct-routes" id="routes" aria-labelledby="routes-title"><div class="ct-route-intro"><p class="ct-kicker">II / A reader’s compass</p><h2 id="routes-title">What brings you<br/><em>to the road?</em></h2><p>You can begin anywhere. These short paths are PointCast’s invitations, not Chaucer’s tale order.</p><form class="ct-js-only" data-routes><fieldset><legend>Choose a reading path</legend>{book.routes.map((route,i)=><label><input type="radio" name="reading-route" value={route.id} checked={i===0}/><span>{route.label}</span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></label>)}</fieldset></form></div><div class="ct-route-result"><span class="ct-compass" aria-hidden="true">✥</span><p class="ct-kicker">YOUR FIRST TWO TALES</p><h3 data-route-title>{firstRoute.label}</h3><p data-route-description>{firstRoute.description}</p><ol data-route-list>{firstRoute.tales.map(id=><li><a href={`#tale-${id}`}>{taleById(id).title}</a></li>)}</ol><div class="ct-nojs-only">{book.routes.slice(1).map(route=><details><summary>{route.label}</summary><p>{route.description}</p><ol>{route.tales.map(id=><li><a href={`#tale-${id}`}>{taleById(id).title}</a></li>)}</ol></details>)}</div><p class="ct-small">A path is a starting point. Every tale stays open to you.</p></div></section>
  <section class="ct-section ct-journey" id="journey" aria-labelledby="journey-title"><header class="ct-section-head"><div><p class="ct-kicker">III / Out of the inn, into the imagination</p><h2 id="journey-title">The road is real.<br/><em>The worlds open out.</em></h2></div><p>A schematic of the frame and its story worlds. No measured distances, reconstructed itinerary, or fixed sequence of tales is implied.</p></header><div class="ct-map" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="ct-map-wide" viewBox="0 0 1000 280" focusable="false"><defs><pattern id="ct-map-stars" width="70" height="70" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"><path d="M10 5v10M5 10h10" stroke="#d8b55e" stroke-width="1" opacity=".5"/></pattern></defs><rect width="1000" height="280" fill="url(#ct-map-stars)"/><path d="M95 188C225 40 335 255 470 138S690 72 835 158" stroke="#d8b55e" stroke-width="5" fill="none" stroke-dasharray="2 10" stroke-linecap="round"/><g stroke="#f3eddb" stroke-width="3" fill="none"><path d="M50 156v-48l44-30 44 30v48M43 108l51-40 51 40M79 156v-30h30v30M56 114h14m47 0h14"/><path d="M320 173v-45h73v45m-78-45 20-27 22 27 22-27 20 27M347 173v-24h20v24"/><path d="M640 112c-18-15-38-8-38 8v67c12-15 27-14 38-8 11-6 26-7 38 8v-67c0-16-20-23-38-8Zm0 0v67"/><path d="M813 162V99h16V69h18v30h15V58h19v41h16v63M823 162v-25h13v25m25 0v-25h13v25m-13-37h13M840 96h9"/></g><g fill="#f3eddb" font-family="Georgia,serif" font-size="20"><text x="40" y="213">Southwark</text><text x="298" y="224">Rochester</text><text x="577" y="230">Story worlds</text><text x="810" y="206">Canterbury</text></g><g fill="#d8b55e"><circle cx="94" cy="183" r="5"/><circle cx="357" cy="196" r="5"/><circle cx="640" cy="210" r="5"/><circle cx="864" cy="180" r="5"/></g></svg><svg class="ct-map-mobile" viewBox="0 0 380 370" focusable="false"><path d="M66 75C335 2 355 183 148 179S13 348 304 315" stroke="#d8b55e" stroke-width="4" fill="none" stroke-dasharray="2 9" stroke-linecap="round"/><g stroke="#f3eddb" stroke-width="2" fill="none"><path d="M40 63V34l26-18 26 18v29M35 35l31-26 31 26M56 63V46h20v17"/><path d="M267 136v-32h48v32m-52-32 14-18 15 18 14-18 15 18M283 136v-15h17v15"/><path d="M86 228c-14-10-28-5-28 7v46c9-10 20-10 28-5 8-5 19-5 28 5v-46c0-12-14-17-28-7Zm0 0v48"/><path d="M280 300v-40h12v-20h13v20h11v-29h13v29h12v40m-33 0v-17h13v17"/></g><g fill="#f3eddb" font-family="Georgia,serif" font-size="17"><text x="22" y="93">Southwark</text><text x="254" y="165">Rochester</text><text x="32" y="309">Story worlds</text><text x="265" y="344">Canterbury</text></g><g fill="#d8b55e"><circle cx="66" cy="74" r="5"/><circle cx="290" cy="147" r="5"/><circle cx="86" cy="291" r="5"/><circle cx="312" cy="315" r="5"/><path d="M179 26v12m-6-6h12m-28 277v12m-6-6h12M30 159v12m-6-6h12" stroke="#d8b55e"/></g></svg></div><div class="ct-journey-grid"><div class="ct-js-only ct-stop-buttons" aria-label="Explore the journey">{book.journey.map((stop,i)=><button type="button" data-journey={stop.id} aria-pressed={i===0}><span>0{i+1}</span>{stop.title}</button>)}</div><article class="ct-js-only ct-stop-copy"><p class="ct-kicker">A NOTE FROM THE ROAD</p><h3 data-journey-title>{firstStop.title}</h3><p data-journey-body>{firstStop.body}</p></article><div class="ct-nojs-only ct-journey-fallback">{book.journey.map(stop=><article><h3>{stop.title}</h3><p>{stop.body}</p></article>)}</div></div></section>
  <section class="ct-section ct-tales" id="tales" aria-labelledby="tales-title"><header class="ct-section-head"><div><p class="ct-kicker">IV / Six illuminated doorways</p><h2 id="tales-title">A tale for<br/><em>every appetite.</em></h2></div><p>Begin with the premise. Sit with the question. Open the ending only when you’re ready.<br/>All summaries and questions here are original PointCast writing.</p></header>
    {book.tales.map((tale,i)=><article class={`ct-tale ct-tale-${tale.id}`} id={`tale-${tale.id}`} data-tale={tale.id} tabindex="-1"><figure><img src={tale.image} srcset={imageSet(tale.id)} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 55vw" width="1440" height="960" alt={tale.alt} loading="lazy" decoding="async"/><figcaption>0{i+1} / AN IMAGINED SCENE</figcaption></figure><div class="ct-tale-copy"><p class="ct-kicker">TOLD BY {tale.pilgrim.toUpperCase()}</p><h3>{tale.title}</h3><p class="ct-tale-premise">{tale.premise}</p><div class="ct-theme-tags">{tale.themes.map(theme=><span>{theme}</span>)}</div><p class="ct-question"><span>A question to carry</span>{tale.question}</p><p class="ct-content-note"><b>Content note.</b> {tale.note}</p><details class="ct-reveal"><summary>Turn the page <span>Plot spoilers inside</span><b aria-hidden="true">+</b></summary><div><p class="ct-kicker">THE STORY OPENS / ORIGINAL SUMMARY</p>{tale.reveal.map(paragraph=><p>{paragraph}</p>)}<a href={tale.source.url}>Read the Middle English at Harvard’s Chaucer site ↗</a><p class="ct-small">{tale.source.lineRange}. Modern translations have separate copyrights; these are our summaries.</p></div></details></div></article>)}
  </section>
  <section class="ct-section ct-threads" id="threads" aria-labelledby="threads-title"><header class="ct-section-head"><div><p class="ct-kicker">V / Follow a thread</p><h2 id="threads-title">Stories talk<br/><em>to other stories.</em></h2></div><p>These pairings are reading interpretations. Compare the storytellers’ values, then let the tales disagree.</p></header><div class="ct-js-only ct-thread-buttons" aria-label="Choose a storytelling connection">{book.connections.map((connection,i)=><button type="button" data-connection={connection.id} aria-pressed={i===0}>{connection.title}</button>)}</div><div class="ct-thread-result"><div class="ct-thread-ornament" aria-hidden="true">❦<br/><span>↙ &nbsp; ↘</span></div><div><h3 data-connection-title>{firstConnection.title}</h3><p data-connection-body>{firstConnection.body}</p><ul data-connection-list>{firstConnection.tales.map(id=><li><a href={`#tale-${id}`}>{taleById(id).title}</a></li>)}</ul></div></div><div class="ct-nojs-only">{book.connections.slice(1).map(connection=><details><summary>{connection.title}</summary><p>{connection.body}</p><ul>{connection.tales.map(id=><li><a href={`#tale-${id}`}>{taleById(id).title}</a></li>)}</ul></details>)}</div></section>
  <section class="ct-section ct-language" id="language" aria-labelledby="language-title"><div><p class="ct-kicker">VI / Four lines, two ways of hearing</p><h2 id="language-title">Hear the<br/><em>company.</em></h2><p>A tiny, curated window into the <i>General Prologue</i>, four separate selections (12, 16, 27, 46). The Middle English is Chaucer’s public-domain text; the accessible gloss is our own, not a published translation.</p><div class="ct-js-only ct-language-buttons" aria-label="Choose passage display"><button type="button" data-language="middle" aria-pressed="false">Middle English</button><button type="button" data-language="gloss" aria-pressed="false">Our gloss</button><button type="button" data-language="both" aria-pressed="true">Side by side</button></div><a class="ct-source-link" href={book.sources.find(source=>source.id==='prologue')?.url}>Check the lines in the source ↗</a></div><div class="ct-poem"><div class="ct-middle"><p class="ct-kicker">MIDDLE ENGLISH / FOUR SEPARATE LINES</p><blockquote lang="enm">{book.lines.map(line=><span><small>{line.number}</small>{line.middle}</span>)}</blockquote></div><div class="ct-gloss"><p class="ct-kicker">POINTCAST’S ACCESSIBLE GLOSS</p><blockquote>{book.lines.map(line=><span><small>{line.number}</small>{line.gloss}</span>)}</blockquote></div></div></section>
  <section class="ct-section ct-margins" aria-labelledby="margin-title"><figure><img src={`${art}marginalia.webp`} width="960" height="960" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Original playful manuscript-style marginalia: a rabbit scribe, a snail knight, birds, and curling painted vines"/></figure><div><p class="ct-kicker">A diversion in the margins</p><h2 id="margin-title">Even the page<br/><em>has opinions.</em></h2><p>Medieval manuscripts could host improbable creatures at their edges. Our newly imagined rabbit and snail are here to interrupt your certainty. <a href="https://www.bl.uk/stories/blogs/posts/knight-v-snail">Visit the British Library’s margins ↗</a></p><details><summary>Can the teller be trusted?</summary><p>The Pardoner condemns greed while confessing his own fraud. A tale can carry a useful warning even when the person delivering it is compromised. Would you trust the lesson, the speaker, both, or neither?</p></details><details><summary>Who gets the last word?</summary><p>The Wife of Bath’s long prologue makes room for experience to argue with inherited authority. When reading, notice who asks a question, who must answer, and who gets to decide whether an answer counts.</p></details><p class="ct-small">A playful modern interpretation, not a reproduction of any historical manuscript.</p></div></section>
  <section class="ct-section ct-frame" id="frame" aria-labelledby="frame-title"><header class="ct-section-head"><div><p class="ct-kicker">Before you go / keep the frame in view</p><h2 id="frame-title">A gathering.<br/><em>Not a single voice.</em></h2></div></header><div class="ct-frame-grid"><article><h3>Chaucer, narrator, pilgrims</h3><p>Geoffrey Chaucer is the author. The traveling “Chaucer” who describes the company is a constructed narrator and fellow pilgrim. The storytellers are fictional voices inside that frame. A character’s claim is not automatically the author’s view.</p></article><article><h3>A deliberately open road</h3><p>The Host proposes two tales from each pilgrim on the way out and two on the return. The surviving work contains 24 tales, some unfinished. Its manuscripts preserve different arrangements; the order between fragments is debated. This page’s six-tale selection is editorial, not a complete or definitive sequence.</p></article><article><h3>Beauty with difficult histories</h3><p>The work contains sexual violence, coercion, misogyny, and antisemitism. In particular, the Prioress’s Tale, outside this selection, tells an antisemitic blood-libel story. Recognizing its historical context does not make that hostility acceptable. Content notes appear before our deeper reveals.</p></article><article><h3>From ink to your screen</h3><p>These original AI-generated illustrations evoke manuscript painting; they are imaginative interpretations, not evidence of Chaucer’s appearance or a reconstructed medieval journey. Our summaries and gloss are new writing. Consult a scholarly edition for the full text and its historical complexity.</p></article></div></section>
  <section class="ct-section ct-sources" id="sources" aria-labelledby="sources-title"><div><p class="ct-kicker">The source shelf / checked October 2026</p><h2 id="sources-title">Every road<br/><em>leaves a trail.</em></h2><p>Scholarship and primary texts inform the frame, tale summaries, and content notes. Interpretive questions and artwork belong to this PointCast edition.</p><a class="ct-button" href="/canterbury.json">Reading paths & sources · JSON ↗</a></div><ol>{book.sources.map(source=><li id={`source-${source.id}`}><a href={source.url}>{source.title}</a><p>{source.use}</p></li>)}</ol></section>
  <footer class="ct-footer"><p><b>THE CANTERBURY TALES</b><br/>An illuminated PointCast companion<br/>Original editorial writing & art / October 2026</p><nav aria-label="Canterbury footer"><a href="/books/">Return to the reading shelf ↗</a><a href="/images/canterbury/provenance.json">Art prompts & provenance ↗</a><a href="#ct-title">Back to the beginning ↑</a></nav><p>No account needed. Take a path, follow a question, leave with a voice still in your ear.</p></footer>
  <p class="ct-sr" data-status role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"></p>
  <dialog class="ct-dialog" data-pilgrim-dialog aria-labelledby="pilgrim-title"><button class="ct-dialog-close" type="button" data-dialog-close autofocus>Close <span aria-hidden="true">×</span></button><div data-dialog-body></div></dialog>
</div>
<script>import book from '../data/canterbury.json';import {initCanterbury} from '../scripts/canterbury.mjs';const setup=()=>initCanterbury(document.querySelector('[data-canterbury]'),book);setup();document.addEventListener('astro:page-load',setup);</script>
</BlockLayout>
